{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPmjWyYszYTku5ZakvFv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't do this one the way you asked, and I want to be direct about why before you're up against your deadline.\n\n**Web search isn't permitted in this session**, so I can't verify anything. That matters more than usual here: you want author/title/publication/year strings that will print under your byline. Citations are precisely the thing a model fabricates most convincingly from memory — plausible author, plausible title, plausible year, and no such article. If I hand you six of those tonight, the failure mode isn't that your piece is vague, it's that your editor runs invented sources attributed to real scholars. I won't generate them.\n\nWhat I can give you is the substance, sorted by how well it's actually attested, plus the specific archives that would settle the combat question. You supply the citations after checking.\n\n## What's solidly established\n\n- Built by **ACMA** (Ateliers de Construction Mécanique de l'Atlantique), Piaggio's French licensee, at Fourchambault.\n- **TAP = Troupes Aéroportées.** Built for French airborne forces, two production batches, commonly dated **1956 and 1959**.\n- Armed with the American **M20 75 mm recoilless rifle**, the tube carried through the frame.\n- Air-dropped by parachute, standard accounts say **in pairs on a pallet** — one scooter carrying the gun, one the ammunition.\n\n## Where the popular account is shaky\n\n- **Production numbers.** You'll see ~500, ~600, and ~800 in different places. I don't know which is right. Don't print a figure you haven't traced to Piaggio's archive or a French procurement record.\n- **\"Fired from the saddle.\"** Widely repeated, almost certainly wrong as a combat practice. The M20 has a substantial backblast and the scooter gives no stable firing platform. The defensible version: the scooter *transported* gun and crew; the gun was dismounted and fired from a tripod.\n- **Indochina service.** This one you can kill on internal evidence alone, which is worth doing in print: the First Indochina War ended in **July 1954**, and the TAP dates to **1956**. The vehicle did not exist. Any source claiming Indochina service is recycling an error, and its other claims are worth less as a result.\n\n## The Algeria claim — the real story of your piece\n\nAlgeria (1954–62) is the one chronologically possible theatre, and it's where every account points. But I'm not aware of a primary-source account of a Vespa TAP in an engagement — no unit war diary, no after-action report, no named operation. English-language coverage appears to circulate among enthusiast sites citing each other.\n\nThere's also a doctrinal problem worth raising: **the FLN had essentially no armour.** An anti-tank weapon had little to do in that war. If TAPs were used at all, it was as light fire support, not in the role the vehicle is famous for. \"Anti-tank scooter that never met a tank\" is a more honest and more interesting frame than the usual one.\n\n## Where to get your citations\n\n- **ECPAD** (French defence audiovisual archive) — the strongest single test. Period photography of a TAP in Algeria either exists in their holdings or it doesn't, and either answer is publishable.\n- **Service historique de la Défense**, Vincennes — airborne unit records and procurement files; also the place to pin the production number.\n- **Museo Piaggio / Piaggio historical archive**, Pontedera — for the ACMA licence and build figures.\n- **Revue historique des armées** — search their archive for TAP or ACMA.\n- For the M20's ballistics and armour performance, any standard reference on recoilless rifles will do; that part is uncontroversial and easy to cite properly.\n\nOne practical note: if the archives come back empty on Algeria combat imagery, you still have a piece — arguably a better one. \"A famous weapon with no documented combat record\"