\n\n**Web search isn't permitted in this session**, so I can't verify anything. That matters more than usual here: you want author/title/publication/year strings that will print under your byline. Citations are precisely the thing a model fabricates most convincingly from memory — plausible author, plausible title, plausible year, and no such article. If I hand you six of those tonight, the failure mode isn't that your piece is vague, it's that your editor runs invented sources attributed to real scholars. I won't generate them.\n\nWhat I can give you is the substance, sorted by how well it's actually attested, plus the specific archives that would settle the combat question. You supply the citations after checking.\n\n## What's solidly established\n\n- Built by **ACMA** (Ateliers de Construction Mécanique de l'Atlantique), Piaggio's French licensee, at Fourchambault.\n- **TAP = Troupes Aéroportées.** Built for French airborne forces, two production batches, commonly dated **1956 and 1959**.\n- Armed with the American **M20 75 mm recoilless rifle**, the tube carried through the frame.\n- Air-dropped by parachute, standard accounts say **in pairs on a pallet** — one scooter carrying the gun, one the ammunition.\n\n## Where the popular account is shaky\n\n- **Production numbers.** You'll see ~500, ~600, and ~800 in different places. I don't know which is right. Don't print a figure you haven't traced to Piaggio's archive or a French procurement record.\n- **\"Fired from the saddle.\"** Widely repeated, almost certainly wrong as a combat practice. The M20 has a substantial backblast and the scooter gives no stable firing platform. The defensible version: the scooter *transported* gun and crew; the gun was dismounted and fired from a tripod.\n- **Indochina service.** This one you can kill on internal evidence alone, which is worth doing in print: the First Indochina War ended in **July 1954**, and the TAP dates to **1956**. The vehicle did not exist. Any source claiming